// TEST: Static Recursion Diagnosed
// ============================================================================
// AN UNBOUNDED STATIC RECURSION IS REFUSED WITH A MESSAGE, NOT WITH A CORPSE.
//
//   `let static X` does not evaluate at compile time: Static evaluation:
//   nesting depth limit exceeded (4096 levels - possible infinite recursion)
//
// What this file pins is REACHABILITY of a guard, which is an unusual thing
// to need a test for, so the history is worth stating.
//
// `StaticEval` has always had a budget named `maxSteps`, always reported
// "step limit exceeded (possible infinite recursion)" on exhausting it, and
// always documented itself as counting steps. It did not count steps: the
// number was threaded as `fuel - 1` into every CHILD of a node, so both
// operands of a `+` received the same remainder from their parent, which
// makes it a bound on evaluation DEPTH wearing a step bound's name. 100,000
// nested evaluator frames exhaust even the 64 MB stack the compiler runs on
// (Runtime.largeStackBytes, installed at Main.fs's runOnLargeStack), so the
// diagnostic was unreachable for precisely the input it was written for. The
// three lines below did not produce it. They produced `Stack overflow.` and
// exit -1 — a StackOverflowException, which .NET does not let anyone catch,
// so no wrapper anywhere in the compiler could have turned it back into a
// diagnostic.
//
// THAT IS WHY THE PIN IS ON THE MESSAGE AND NOT MERELY ON REJECTION. A probe
// that only asserted "this program is refused" would have passed in the
// crashing regime too, for a sufficiently generous definition of refused:
// the process does not accept the program, it simply also does not survive
// it. The `// ERROR:` line below is what distinguishes the two, and the
// diagnostics corpus is strict in both directions (Test_DiagCorpus), so it
// also asserts that nothing ELSE is reported — the program is otherwise
// well-formed, and `bomb` is a perfectly legal declaration.
//
// The budget is now two numbers, because there are two ways to run away and
// neither bound implies the other: STEPS (`maxSteps`, shared across sibling
// subexpressions — the work bound) and DEPTH (`maxDepth`, the evaluator's
// own nesting — the survival bound). It is the depth guard that fires here,
// correctly: `bomb` recurses without widening, so it runs out of stack
// budget long before it runs out of work budget. The step bound is what
// catches the other shape, `f(n) = g(n-1) + g(n-1)`, which does 2^depth work
// inside any depth limit and which a depth bound alone would wave through;
// Test_Static pins that half, since no corpus program can express "returned
// eventually, but should not have".
//
// The counting layer's twin is index-types/156, where the same runaway sits
// in a `where` conjunct and is caught per cell under the much smaller
// `cellBudget`.
// ============================================================================
static function bomb(n: Int) -> Int = bomb(n + 1)

let static X = bomb(0)

function main() -> Int = X
// ERROR: BL3999
// ERROR-CONTAINS: nesting depth limit exceeded (4096 levels
